The wide angle: a Hi-1337 driver built from the phone's own tables

WHAT IT BRINGS
--------------
1. drivers/media/i2c/hi1337.c, a v4l2_subdev for the 13 MP SK Hynix Hi-1337 that
   lives at 0x21 on CCI bus 14, ported from s5k3t2.c in this very tree because
   the two are wired the same way: four D-PHY lanes.
2. The sensor node in the device tree, under cci1_i2c0.

WHERE EACH PIECE COMES FROM
---------------------------
The tables -1457 initialisation writes and 87 per mode- come from THIS module's
factory file, com.qti.sensormodule.j20c_sunny_hi1337_ultra.bin, read with
surya/camara/leer-sensormodule.py --tablas.

And the identification is MEASURED on the chip, not copied from a sibling driver:

    0x0716 = 0x1337     the identification
    0x0b12 = 4208       output width
    0x0b14 = 3120       output height

The last two match what the factory tables say, and that agreement along two
independent paths is what confirms the tables belong to this sensor and not to
another.

WHY THAT MATTERS SO MUCH
------------------------
Because the fault that blocked this investigation for entire rounds was exactly
the opposite: the scanner read the identification at 0x0f16 because that is what
the mainline hi846 driver says, taking for granted that another SK Hynix sensor
would have it in the same place. It does not. 0000 was read over and over and
interpreted as "the sensor is dead", when it meant "that register does not exist
here".

Hence the STRUCTURE is copied from this sibling driver and not the constants.

A SCARE, AND AN ATTRIBUTION OF MINE THAT WAS FALSE
--------------------------------------------------
When the first version of this patch was installed the phone did not come back:
no ping, no ssh, and over USB only U-Boot's fastboot gadget. I attributed it to
the sensor node carrying

    assigned-clocks = <&camcc CAMCC_MCLK3_CLK>;

which is fault number 1 of the scanner -the core applies it in
of_clk_set_defaults() when REGISTERING the node, with no driver involved- and
which hung the boot there with MCLK2.

IT WAS A LIE, and it is worth putting in writing. When the phone came back, the
state was: /boot dated from r155, no hi1337.ko on disk and apk saying the
installed one was still r155. So r157 NEVER got installed and the kernel that
failed to boot was r155, which had already booted fine before. That
assigned-clocks never ran at all.

The most likely cause is this phone's known boot failure, which this very
repository has measured at roughly one in four reboots.

And I did not realise the installation had failed because of a handling mistake
of mine: I chained "instalar-kernel ... | grep version:", and that pipe swallows
the exit code. The script has a version guard and a check that it actually wrote,
and I nullified them by filtering its output. You have to look at the artefact's
exit code, not just at the line you happen to care about.

BOTH CHANGES STAY, but on their own merit
-----------------------------------------
1. No assigned-clocks: the frequency is set by the driver with clk_set_rate() in
   the probe. Not because it hung -it did not-, but because the scanner's
   precedent with MCLK2 is real and this path avoids the risk entirely.

2. No MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE: with it, udev loads the driver by itself as soon as
   the node appears, that is, during boot and with nobody watching. This driver
   is on its first version: if it blows up, it has to blow up with the phone
   already booted. It is loaded by hand with modprobe hi1337, just like the
   scanner. It is the rule this project already had written down.

IT HAS TO BE ENABLED IN THE CONFIG, AND THAT DOES NOT LIVE HERE
---------------------------------------------------------------
The patch adds the VIDEO_HI1337 option to the Kconfig, but does not enable it:
the kernel configuration is not in this repository, it is in the aport, in

    device/community/linux-postmarketos-qcom-sm7150/config-postmarketos-qcom-sm7150.aarch64

and there you have to put

    CONFIG_VIDEO_HI1337=m

The first build of this patch came out without the module because that had not
been done: the device tree node appeared in the DTB and the .ko did not exist.
You can see it by looking at the apk, which is exactly what verifying the
artefact before installing it is for.

WHAT IT DOES NOT BRING, AND ON PURPOSE
--------------------------------------
1. It does not touch camss. Patch 0051 wrote down why: if a port is declared in
   camss and the sensor hanging off it does not come up, the async notifier waits
   forever and /dev/media0 is lost -and with it the front camera, which does
   work-. So first you check that the sensor comes up, powers on and identifies
   itself; the CSI wiring goes separately and knowingly.

   Consequence: with this patch the sensor does NOT stream yet. You can see it
   come up in dmesg, and that is what this version wants to demonstrate.

2. It does not expose exposure or gain controls. It is not known for certain
   which registers the Hi-1337 has them in: they are somewhere in the 0x0200
   block -the per-channel digital gains are localised, at 0x0214..0x021a, and
   come from the factory file itself- but guessing which is which only serves to
   write to the wrong register, and that breaks the link in a way that looks like
   a CSI problem. It sticks with what the tables set.

THE LINK FREQUENCY IS PROVISIONAL
---------------------------------
In the full mode's PLL, the multiplier of the first chain (0x0736) is 90, which
with INCK = 19.2 MHz gives a 1728 MHz VCO and therefore an 864 MHz link clock.
That comfortably matches the minimum the full mode requires (493 MHz), but the
exact PLL structure of this sensor is not documented and it has NOT been possible
to verify it by a second path, which is how the front camera's was validated.

That is why it goes in as a module parameter, link_freq_hz, and can be swept
without recompiling.

